  "summary": "Both previous proposals miss a clean middle ground that avoids os.execv's flaws without the architectural overhead of a supervisor process. The solution is a restart loop inside run_tui() itself: wrap the existing app.run() call in a while loop, and when pipeline/*.py changes are detected (via the existing _check_for_changes polling, extended to also track pipeline code mtimes), set a flag like self._restart_requested = True and call self.exit(). Textual's exit() properly restores terminal state (alternate screen, raw mode, mouse capture) and runs through the normal shutdown lifecycle, after which _release_lock() executes as it already does at line 4076. The loop then re-acquires the lock, creates a fresh PipelineApp instance, and calls app.run() again — guaranteeing completely fresh module imports with zero stale state, no terminal corruption, and no lock leaks. This requires roughly 5 lines of changes to run_tui() plus a small mtime check addition to _check_for_changes(). No new dependencies, no new processes, no architectural changes. The only UX consideration is the brief screen flash on restart, which can be mitigated by checking code mtimes only every 30-60 seconds (code changes during active development are infrequent enough that this lag is acceptable) and by optionally persisting minimal UI state (active board, selected slug) to a temp file so the restarted app can restore the user's position."
